MIOpen (pinned tag rocm-6.4.4): gfx803 workaround -- bound how many distinct
dynamic-reduction programs MIOpen keeps resident on a shared Handle at once,
to avoid miopenReduceTensor's intermittent wrong-sum bug.

EXPERIMENTAL / UNDER VALIDATION -- not yet shipped. Wired into the Dockerfile
only behind --build-arg ENABLE_REDUCE_BOUND=1 and must pass the real ORT
ReductionOpTest suite AND a before/after perf benchmark before shipping. Revert
the Dockerfile wiring (not this file) if it fails either gate.

This is NOT a root-cause fix: the mechanism is a gfx803 GPU code-object /
instruction-execution defect tied to accumulated distinct JIT programs (see
KERNEL_BUGS.md "The ReduceSum kernel-cache mystery"). Proven NOT a data or
descriptor race: AMD_SERIALIZE_KERNEL=3 and handle.Finish() are inert, a
never-freed workspace pool is inert, the failure is stable per accumulated
state (re-dispatch on the same handle still fails), and any added device write
masks it. The confirmed eliminator is "few distinct reduce programs resident
during a dispatch". This patch keeps only the most-recent-N reduce programs
resident (LRU, Default N=2, tunable via MIOPEN_DEBUG_REDUCE_PROGRAM_BOUND; 0
disables, sweeping N needs just one build) so repeated same-shape calls stay
cache-hit (fast path preserved) while dispatches run in the low-accumulation
state that is always correct. Requires handle.Finish() before eviction
(ClearProgram can release a GPU code object a just-launched kernel may still be
executing from) and clears every network_config that referenced an evicted
program so no cached Kernel points into the freed code object.

